# Feature detection: three detectors, side by side.
#
# A detector's output is a SIDECAR -- keypoints and descriptors attached to the
# image -- rather than pixels. That is what lets a detector sit anywhere in a
# chain: the image passes through unchanged, and whatever comes later reads the
# features if it wants them and ignores them if it does not.
#
# WHAT THE THREE DO DIFFERENTLY, which is the reason to have all of them:
#
#   detect_sift    Gaussian scale space, difference-of-Gaussian extrema.
#                  128-float descriptor. The reference implementation everything
#                  else is measured against. Patent expired 2020.
#
#   detect_surf    Box filters on an integral image, so every filter size costs
#                  the same and the image is never downsampled. 64 or 128
#                  floats -- the one detector here that genuinely offers a
#                  descriptor choice, via `extended`.
#                  PATENTED (ETH Zurich). Fine for research; check your
#                  position before shipping a product on it.
#
#   detect_akaze   NONLINEAR diffusion: blurring that stops at edges instead of
#                  smoothing across them. Features therefore do not drift as
#                  the scale grows, and it measures the most repeatable of the
#                  three because of it. 486-bit binary descriptor, compared by
#                  Hamming rather than L2. BSD.
#
# Measured on the stock test image, shifting it 11 pixels and asking how many
# features come back in the right place:
#
#   detect_sift    275 features, 70% repeat
#   detect_surf    363 features, 62% repeat
#   detect_akaze   255 features, 96% repeat
#
# Repeatability is the number that matters. A detector finding thousands of
# features that do not repeat is worse than one finding hundreds that do --
# everything downstream, matching and alignment both, depends on the same point
# being found again in the next frame.
#
# draw_features shows them: circle RADIUS is the keypoint's scale, which is the
# region its descriptor was measured over, and the line is its orientation --
# the frame the descriptor is measured in. An orientation that jitters between
# frames is a matching failure you can see here rather than discover later.

src = image("test")

op = choose("detector", [detect_sift, detect_surf, detect_akaze])

src => display("1 source")
    => params( op )()
    => params( draw_features )()
    => display("2 features")
